            <ArticleTitle>Evolution: Selfing Takes Species Down Stebbins's Blind Alley.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>R61-R63</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0960-9822(16)31383-5</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.cub.2016.11.030</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Shifts from outcrossing to selfing have occurred thousands of times across the tree of life. By reducing the size of the gene pool, selfing should limit adaptive potential. A refreshing empirical experiment with snails supports this long-standing hypothesis.</AbstractText>
